<div style="background-color: #f0f8ff; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Fun Outdoors!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Like to Swim.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Text: Summarize short stories using drawings or pictures.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Concept: Show a connection between class concepts and personal experiences.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can summarize short stories using simple drawings (e.g., draw a sunny scene and a swimmer).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can show a connection between class concepts and personal experiences by drawing or gesturing (e.g., drawing themselves running and saying, "I run fast.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can connect familiar actions to new words (e.g., saying "I play." and drawing a ball).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 25%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays flashcards of outdoor activities (e.g., swimming, running, playing soccer, jumping). The teacher asks, "What do you like to do outdoors?" and points to each picture, eliciting "I like to swim!" or "I like to run!" from students. Students show thumbs up for activities they like.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher models "I like to swim. I swim fast!" while doing a swimming motion. The teacher points to a picture of someone running and says, "I like to run. I run quickly!" The teacher emphasizes the /r/ sound in "run" and the /s/ sound in "swim" and has students repeat chorally.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of swimming) "Is this 'run' or 'swim'?" (Expected answer: "Swim!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher makes a fast running motion) "Do I run slowly or fast?" (Expected answer: "Fast!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">A short, illustrated story or comic strip panel (narrative excerpt) featuring a character describing their favorite outdoor activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text (displayed with simple drawings):<br>
                <b>Panel 1:</b> Picture of a girl in a pool.<br>
                <i>"Hello! My name is Mia. I like to swim.</i><br>
                <b>Panel 2:</b> Picture of the girl swimming quickly.<br>
                <i>I swim quickly in the big pool. It is fun!</i><br>
                <b>Panel 3:</b> Picture of a boy with a ball.<br>
                <i>My friend Leo likes to play soccer. He plays fast.</i></p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Pragmatic: Expressing preferences for activities ("I like to run. It's fun.").</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher asks, "What does Mia like to do?" (pointing to Mia's picture). Students respond, "Swim!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> The teacher shows a picture of "playing soccer" and asks, "Who likes to play soccer? Mia or Leo?" Students point to Leo's picture.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Grammar: Subject + verb + adverb (e.g., "I run fast."), Present simple (e.g., "I love to run.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Vocabulary: run, jump, swim, play, fast, slow, happy, fun, like, love.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Pronunciation: /r/ in "run", /s/ in "swim".</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence to Picture Matching:</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet containing simple sentences (e.g., "I run fast.", "I swim slowly.", "I play happily.") and a selection of pictures depicting these actions. Students draw lines to match each sentence to the correct picture. This practices summarizing the sentence visually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Drawing the Action:</b> The teacher gives students sentence starters like "I like to ____. I ____ (adverb)." (e.g., "I like to jump. I jump high.") on a mini-whiteboard or paper. Students choose a verb and adverb, complete the sentence orally, and then draw a simple picture that summarizes the action. This connects new words to visual representation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Picture Talk:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student shows their drawing from the previous activity to their partner. The partner identifies the action and says the sentence (e.g., "You like to jump. You jump high!"). The original student confirms. This requires students to mediate their partner's drawing into spoken language.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Task Format:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Individual drawing of a comic strip panel, focusing on the 21st Century Skills Project: "Digital Sports Day."</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains that students will create one panel for our "Digital Sports Day" comic strip/video project.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a blank "comic panel" sheet (a framed box) and drawing materials.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw a picture of their *favorite* outdoor activity (e.g., themselves swimming, running, playing soccer with a ball).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, they write a simple sentence or two describing their activity, using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "I love to swim fast!" or "I like to play soccer. It is fun!" The teacher provides sentence frames on the board as a scaffold.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students will be mediating their personal experience into a visual and textual summary for the project.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students briefly present their comic strip panel drawing from Stage 4 to the class or a small group, verbally summarizing their favorite activity using 1-2 sentences. The teacher circulates and observes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example: Student shows drawing of themselves swimming and says, "This is me. I love to swim quickly! It is fun!"</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear /r/ in "run" and /s/ in "swim".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates at least one verb (e.g., swim, run, play) and one adjective/adverb (e.g., fast, fun, quickly).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Drawing:</b> Drawing clearly represents the chosen activity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Structure:</b> Uses simple sentences like "I like/love to (verb) (adverb)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to Personal Experience:</b> Clearly shows their own preference.</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I draw my favorite outdoor activity?" (Students can draw a happy face or sad face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'I love to swim' or 'I like to run'?" (Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (8 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun for you today in English class?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new action or word did you draw or say?"</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this mediation-focused lesson? The use of drawings to summarize and connect personal experiences was highly engaging for 2nd graders. Students were able to articulate their preferences with simple sentences and show clear connections between visuals and language. The project-based output for "Digital Sports Day" motivated them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next unit/theme? Some students still need more practice with adverb placement or choosing appropriate adverbs (e.g., confusing "high" with "quickly"). More explicit modeling of the /s/ sound, perhaps through a short chant, could be beneficial. For the next theme, ensure ample visual support and TPR are integrated, especially when introducing new action verbs and associated descriptive words.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson concludes the "Fun Outdoors! / I Like to Swim" theme. The next lesson will begin a new thematic unit, building upon the established foundation of expressing preferences and describing actions, potentially in a different context or introducing new vocabulary and concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ (2 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>